          body:
            "An [[incidental-finding|incidental finding]] is an abnormality found on a test done for another reason, which did not cause the symptoms that prompted the test. A population study that scanned 2,000 adults without neurological symptoms found incidental abnormalities in a substantial minority: asymptomatic infarcts were the most common, followed by small aneurysms and benign tumors, mostly meningiomas, and the number rose with age. A meta-analysis confirmed that incidental brain MRI findings are common, become more common with age, and are detected more often with higher resolution sequences.\n\nFindings you will meet often:\n\n- Nonspecific [[white-matter-hyperintensity|white matter hyperintensities]]: small bright spots on T2 and FLAIR. They become more frequent with age and with vascular risk factors such as hypertension, and they usually reflect [[small-vessel-disease|small vessel disease]]. They are not MS.\n- Small unruptured [[intracranial-aneurysm|intracranial aneurysms]]: usually silent; they need specialist risk assessment, not dismissal.\n- [[arachnoid-cyst|Arachnoid cysts]] and [[pineal-cyst|pineal cysts]]: fluid-filled and usually harmless.\n- Low-lying cerebellar tonsils ([[chiari-malformation|Chiari malformation]] on imaging): often found without symptoms; they explain symptoms only when the clinical picture fits, such as headache brought on by coughing.\n- Small [[meningioma|meningiomas]]: often watched rather than treated when small and silent.\n- [[radiologically-isolated-syndrome|Radiologically isolated syndrome]]: lesions typical of MS in shape and location in someone who has never had MS symptoms. This is different from nonspecific white matter hyperintensities and needs an MS specialist.\n\nBlood tests behave the same way. A panel of many tests, each with a reference range that by definition excludes some healthy people, will often flag at least one value outside its range in a well person. The more tests you order without a question in mind, the more incidental results you will find.",
