            simpler: "If a test disagrees with the patient, ask how likely the disease was beforehand. A positive test in someone who probably does not have the disease is often wrong. A negative test in someone who probably does have it is often wrong too.",
            mechanism: "Post-test odds equal pretest odds multiplied by the likelihood ratio. A weak positive (a faint line blot band, a low serum titer) has a modest likelihood ratio, so in a low-probability patient the post-test probability stays low. A test done too early has lower sensitivity, so its negative likelihood ratio is closer to 1 and a negative result barely lowers a high pretest probability.",
            compare: "Strong picture, weak test: believe the picture and act on it (treat the stroke, manage the Guillain-Barre syndrome) while repeating or improving the test. Weak picture, weak positive: believe the picture and confirm before labeling. Strong test, unexpected picture (a high titer CSF antibody in an atypical syndrome): take it seriously and rethink the syndrome, because a strong result can outweigh a weak prior.",
            deeper: "Discordance is not always error. Sometimes the test is right and the clinical picture was misread; sometimes both are right and the patient has two problems. The aim is not to dismiss the inconvenient result but to explain it. If you cannot explain a discordant result, say so in the notes and plan how to resolve it.",
