          body:
            "Look for a reason the test and the patient disagree:\n\n- Timing: the test was done too early (CSF protein in the first days of Guillain-Barre syndrome, diffusion MRI in the first hours of a small brainstem stroke) or after treatment that changes it (steroids, plasma exchange, or immunoglobulin lowering antibody titers).\n- Technique: the method has known false positives (line blot, low titer serum cell-based assay), the wrong specimen was tested (serum when CSF is more reliable), or the sample was affected by something else (recent exercise or injections for CK).\n- Interpretation: a normal variant was read as abnormal (benign EEG patterns), or a value just outside a reference range was treated as disease.\n\nThen choose the response that fits:\n\n- Repeat the test at a better time: CSF after the first week in suspected Guillain-Barre syndrome, CK after a period without strenuous exercise or injections, MRI a few days later after a clinically clear stroke.\n- Use a better test or specimen: CSF rather than serum for NMDA receptor antibodies, a tissue-based assay to confirm a line blot band, methylmalonic acid and homocysteine for a borderline B12, nerve conduction studies to support Guillain-Barre syndrome.\n- Seek another opinion: an expert re-read of the EEG or MRI, or a discussion with the laboratory.\n- Act on the clinical picture when it is strong and delay is dangerous: a patient with clear stroke or progressing weakness is managed for that condition while the test question is resolved.\n\nAnd do not let a weak positive start a new diagnosis by itself: no immunotherapy for an isolated low titer serum antibody in the wrong syndrome, and no antiseizure medication for an overread EEG in someone whose history describes syncope.",
